We now introduce the central formal construct of this paper: the Effective Assembly Index for Mind Recognition (EAIMR). The aim of EAIMR is to distinguish systems that merely accumulate complexity from those that organize complexity in a mind-like manner.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Effective Assembly Index for Mind Recognition]
For a system, structure, or process </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, define
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{EAIMR}(x)
=
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{A(x)}{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\left</span></span><span>(R(x)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>P(x)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>D(x)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\right</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{1/3}} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span> C(x),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>A(x)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is the assembly index of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>R(x)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> quantifies reuse,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>P(x)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> quantifies parallelism,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>D(x)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> quantifies degeneracy,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>C(x)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> quantifies coherence.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

The geometric mean in the denominator ensures that effective assembly is maximized when reuse, parallelism, and degeneracy are balanced. Extreme specialization along a single axis cannot compensate for deficiencies in the others. The coherence term amplifies systems whose parts compose into a stable whole.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Reuse}

Reuse </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>R(x)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> measures the extent to which substructures are invoked multiple times across the system. High reuse reflects abstraction: the ability to deploy the same structural component in many contexts without duplication.

In biological cognition, reuse appears in neural circuits repurposed across tasks. In artificial systems, reuse appears in shared parameters, functions, and modules. Systems with high assembly but low reuse expend complexity wastefully, rebuilding structure rather than abstracting it.

Reuse is therefore a primary mechanism by which compression becomes productive rather than lossy.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Parallelism}

Parallelism </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>P(x)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> measures the degree to which construction or operation can occur simultaneously. Parallel systems compress time and energy by exploiting partial independence without fragmenting into incoherent subsystems.

Parallelism is essential for real-time interaction with complex environments. Sequential systems may be expressive but are brittle and slow. Minds, by contrast, distribute processing across multiple pathways while maintaining coordination.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Degeneracy}

Degeneracy </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>D(x)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> captures the availability of distinct structures capable of fulfilling similar functional roles. Unlike redundancy, which duplicates identical components, degeneracy supports robustness and adaptability by allowing heterogeneous components to substitute for one another.

Biological nervous systems exhibit high degeneracy: multiple neural pathways can realize similar behaviors. This diversity allows systems to absorb damage and adapt without catastrophic failure.

Degeneracy thus discounts assembly by accounting for functional overlap that reduces effective complexity.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Coherence}

Coherence </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>C(x)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> measures the degree to which a system maintains global consistency under perturbation. A coherent system localizes disturbances rather than amplifying them across the entire structure.

Conceptually, coherence captures integration without rigidity. Systems with low coherence fragment under stress; systems with excessive rigidity cannot adapt. Minds occupy an intermediate regime in which local change is possible without global collapse.

Operationally, coherence may be estimated by measuring perturbation propagation: the extent to which local interventions induce widespread effects.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Intelligence as a Compression Regime}

Under the EAIMR framework, intelligence corresponds to a narrow corridor in structural space. Systems with low assembly lack expressive power. Systems with high assembly but low reuse, parallelism, or degeneracy waste complexity. Systems with high efficiency but low coherence fragment.

Mind-like systems are those in which irreducible assembly is balanced by structural efficiency and stabilized by coherence. Intelligence, in this sense, is compression that persists.

