Recent work on the mathematical foundations of intelligence characterizes 
intelligence as governed by two core principles: parsimony and 
self-consistency. Parsimony refers to the discovery of low-dimensional, 
predictive structure in experience, while self-consistency requires that 
internal representations be stable enough to support simulation and 
counterfactual reasoning.

Spherepop OS does not attempt to implement intelligence directly. Rather, 
it provides a deterministic semantic substrate in which such principles 
can be realized, inspected, and composed. Parsimony corresponds, at the 
system level, to the emergence of reusable equivalence classes and shared 
substructures in the event-derived assembly graph. Self-consistency is 
realized through strict replay determinism, total causal order, and the 
separation of authoritative state from derived views.

From this perspective, memorization corresponds to unstructured growth of 
the event log, while abstraction corresponds to transformations that 
increase reuse and stability of semantic structure across contexts. 
Speculative branches and seekable time provide the formal machinery 
required for simulation without causal interference, a prerequisite for 
model-based reasoning.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
<p data-start="4103" data-end="4165">That’s it.<br data-start="4113" data-end="4116">
No citations required unless you want them later.</p>
