C4.04237 6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-latex"><span><span>Because the event log records the full construction history of semantic 
state, it distinguishes between mere accumulation and genuine abstraction. 
Appending events that introduce novel objects without inducing equivalence 
or reuse increases historical complexity without increasing semantic power. 
By contrast, events that induce stable equivalence or collapse reduce the 
number of distinct representatives required to sustain the same observable 
relations. This distinction is not inferred post hoc; it is directly legible 
from the structure of the log itself.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
<p data-start="3903" data-end="3970">This reinforces that abstraction is <em data-start="3939" data-end="3951">detectable</em>, not interpretive.</p>
