</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{abstract}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ==================================================</span></span><span>
Spherepop OS is a semantic operating system whose primary abstraction is not
the process, file, or thread, but a deterministic, append-only relational
event substrate. The system is designed to support collaborative, time-aware,
and introspectable computation by enforcing strict causal ordering,
replayability, and separation between authoritative state transitions and
derived views. Geometry, layout, speculation, and visualization are treated
as non-causal metadata layered atop an immutable event log.

Beyond systems concerns, this architecture makes explicit a structural
distinction between mere accumulation of complexity and the emergence of
reusable abstraction. By recording construction history rather than final
state alone, Spherepop OS provides a concrete semantic substrate in which
parsimony and self-consistency—widely argued to underlie intelligent
organization—can be realized, inspected, and reasoned about without
appealing to behavior or linguistic surface form.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{abstract}

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ==================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Introduction}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ==================================================</span></span><span>

Modern operating systems and data platforms are optimized for efficiency,
throughput, and resource isolation. Their abstractions—processes, files,
transactions—are rooted in imperative control and mutable global state.
While effective for single-user and single-machine computation, these
architectures exhibit deep limitations when extended to collaborative,
distributed, or semantically rich environments.

In particular, traditional systems struggle with:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> exact reproducibility of global state,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> principled historical inspection,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> collaborative reasoning over time,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> separation between authoritative causality and derived interpretation.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

At the same time, contemporary discussions of intelligence increasingly
emphasize structural properties rather than surface behavior. Intelligence
is argued to depend not merely on compression or prediction, but on the
ability to form stable, reusable abstractions that support simulation and
counterfactual reasoning </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{ma2024}. These properties are difficult to
study in systems whose internal state evolution is opaque or irreversibly
mutated.

Spherepop OS is proposed as a response to both challenges. It is a
deterministic semantic operating system in which all authoritative state
changes are recorded in a single, totally ordered event log. From this log,
all semantic structure—objects, relations, equivalence classes, regions—is
derived by replay. The log is the sole authority; all other structures are
views.

This design enables a shift in perspective: from systems defined by their
current configuration to systems defined by their construction history.
That shift makes explicit distinctions that are otherwise blurred, such as
the difference between compression and abstraction, or between memorization
and understanding.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ==================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Motivation and Design Goals}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ==================================================</span></span><span>

Spherepop OS is guided by the following design goals:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{enumerate}[label=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{G</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\arabic</span></span><span>*.}]
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Deterministic Replay}. Any system state must be reproducible
exactly from an authoritative log.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Total Causal Order}. All authoritative state transitions must
admit a single, global ordering.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{View--Cause Separation}. Observations, visualizations, and
speculative reasoning must not interfere with authoritative state.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Semantic Primacy}. Relations, equivalence, and meaning precede
processes and files as first-class concepts.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Collaborative Introspection}. Multiple observers may join,
leave, rewind, and speculate without destabilizing the system.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{enumerate}

These goals rule out large classes of conventional kernel, database, and
distributed architectures. In particular, they preclude designs in which
mutable state is the primary authority or in which observation and causation
are entangled.

Beyond correctness and collaboration, Spherepop OS is motivated by a deeper
theoretical concern: how to distinguish systems that merely accumulate
complexity from those that organize complexity in a reusable, resilient, and
self-stabilizing manner. This distinction cannot be made reliably from
external behavior alone; it requires access to construction history and
internal structure.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ==================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Parsimony, Self-Consistency, and Semantic Assembly}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ==================================================</span></span><span>

Recent work on the mathematical foundations of intelligence argues that
intelligent systems are governed by two core principles: parsimony and
self-consistency </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{ma2024}. Parsimony refers to the discovery of
low-dimensional, predictive structure—compression that avoids combinatorial
explosion. Self-consistency requires that internal representations remain
stable enough to support simulation, counterfactual reasoning, and reliable
prediction.

A central open question in this framework concerns the distinction between
compression and abstraction. Compression reduces the quantity of stored
information—fewer bits, fewer symbols, fewer recorded events. Abstraction,
by contrast, reduces the number of distinct semantic representatives required
to support the same range of predictions and actions.

Spherepop OS makes this distinction explicit at the level of construction
history. A system may compress by recording fewer events or encoding them
more efficiently, without altering its semantic structure. Abstraction
occurs only when multiple concrete constructions become identified as
instances of a single canonical representative, enabling reuse across
contexts. This is visible in the log as the stabilization of equivalence
classes and the collapse of regions without loss of relational consequence.

In this sense, compression concerns the </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{length} of history, while
abstraction concerns the </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{topology} of history. The former may reduce
storage or bandwidth; the latter increases the system’s capacity to support
future reasoning with fewer semantic degrees of freedom.

Spherepop OS does not implement intelligence directly. Rather, it provides a
deterministic semantic substrate in which parsimony and self-consistency can
be realized, inspected, and composed. Memorization corresponds to
unstructured growth of the event log. Understanding corresponds to patterns
of reuse, equivalence, and stability that reshape the assembly graph itself.

