4.04237 6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-latex"><span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ==================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Diffs and Incremental Observation}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ==================================================</span></span><span>

While the event log is the sole authoritative record, observers require
efficient mechanisms for tracking state evolution without full replay.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Diff]
A </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{diff} is a derived, non-authoritative description of the semantic
changes induced by applying a single event to a kernel state.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

Diffs are produced by the kernel as a byproduct of event application. They
may include notifications of object creation, relation changes, equivalence
updates, or metadata modifications.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{axiom}[Diff Non-Authority]
Diffs are observational artifacts. They do not constitute state, are not
logged, and have no effect on future kernel execution.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{axiom}

This axiom is critical: it ensures that observation never feeds back into
causation. The system is therefore immune to observer-induced drift.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Incremental Observation}

Observers may maintain local replicas of semantic state by applying diffs in
sequence order.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proposition}[Incremental Equivalence]
Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be the kernel state after replaying events </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dots</span></span><span>,e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\hat</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be the observer state obtained by applying diffs
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dots</span></span><span>,d</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> starting from an empty state. Then </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\hat</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is
semantically equivalent to </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proposition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proof}
Each diff </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is derived deterministically from the kernel transition
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{i-1} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\xrightarrow</span></span><span>{e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. Since kernel transitions are pure
and diffs preserve all semantic information, incremental application yields
a state equivalent to replay.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proof}

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ==================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Snapshots as Derived Views}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ==================================================</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Snapshot]
A </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{snapshot} is a complete serialization of kernel state derived by
replaying a prefix of the event log.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

Snapshots are used for bootstrapping new observers, historical inspection,
and checkpointing. Crucially, snapshots are never authoritative.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{axiom}[Snapshot Non-Authority]
Snapshots introduce no semantic information beyond what is contained in the
log prefix they represent.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{axiom}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proposition}[Snapshot Purity]
For any snapshot </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> derived from log prefix </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>[1..k]</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, there exists a
unique kernel state </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> such that </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a serialization of
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proposition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Replay Equivalence}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{theorem}[Replay Equivalence]
Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ell</span></span><span> = e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dots</span></span><span>,e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be an event sequence. Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{replay}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>
be the state obtained by replaying </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ell</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> from the empty state. Let
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{incr}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be the state obtained by applying diffs
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dots</span></span><span>,d</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. Then </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{replay}} = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{incr}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> up to
semantic equivalence.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{theorem}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proof}
Both constructions apply the same deterministic kernel transitions in the
same order. Since diffs preserve semantic content and introduce no new
authority, the resulting states coincide.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proof}

This theorem guarantees that all observers—whether replay-based or
diff-based—converge on the same semantic world.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ==================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Seekable Time and Historical Inspection}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ==================================================</span></span><span>

A defining feature of Spherepop OS is explicit support for time navigation.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Seek Operation]
A </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{seek} operation </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{seek}(k)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> instantiates an ephemeral kernel
state by replaying the event log prefix </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>[1..k]</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

Seek operations are read-only and isolated from the live kernel.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{axiom}[Temporal Isolation]
Seek operations produce no side effects on the authoritative kernel state
and emit no diffs.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{axiom}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{corollary}
Historical inspection cannot affect present or future state.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{corollary}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Temporal Monotonicity}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proposition}[Monotone Time]
The authoritative sequence identifier is strictly increasing. No operation
may decrease or rewrite time.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proposition}

This enforces a strong notion of causal consistency: the past is immutable,
and the future is always constructed relative to a fixed history.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ==================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Self-Consistency and Simulation Capacity}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ==================================================</span></span><span>

The architectural commitments above jointly enforce a strong notion of
self-consistency. The system is always able to reconstruct its own state
from first principles.

This property aligns closely with what </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textcite</span></span><span>{ma2023} identifies as a
core requirement of intelligence: the ability to maintain a world model
that is sufficiently consistent to support accurate simulation.

In Spherepop OS, self-consistency is not an emergent property but a
structural invariant:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> The event log provides a complete causal history.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Replay reconstructs state without loss.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Snapshots and diffs are derivable views.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Time navigation preserves causal order.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proposition}[Strong Self-Consistency]
At any time </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, the semantic state of Spherepop OS can be reconstructed
exactly from the event log prefix </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>[1..t]</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proposition}

This guarantees that the system can, in principle, simulate any past state
and evaluate counterfactual futures via speculative branches (introduced in
the next section).

Crucially, this notion of self-consistency operates </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{below language}.
Unlike large language models, which compress already-abstracted linguistic
artifacts, Spherepop OS operates at the level of semantic construction
itself. This positions it as a pre-linguistic substrate—precisely the level
at which </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textcite</span></span><span>{ma2023} locates animal and human intelligence.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% --- CONTINUE NEXT MESSAGE ---</span></span><span>
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
