</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Kernel Semantics and Deterministic Execution}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ==================================================</span></span><span>

At the heart of Spherepop OS lies a deliberately minimal kernel. Unlike
traditional kernels, which manage processes, memory, and scheduling, the
Spherepop kernel performs a single task: it deterministically interprets the
authoritative event log. All higher-level phenomena—state, structure,
abstraction, and even time—emerge from this interpretation.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Kernel]
The kernel is the deterministic transition function that maps an event-log
prefix to a semantic state. It possesses no mutable internal state beyond
that which is derivable from replay, and it produces no side effects beyond
derived views.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

This definition is intentionally austere. The kernel is not a scheduler, a
memory manager, or a policy engine. It is a semantic interpreter whose sole
responsibility is to ensure that identical histories yield identical worlds.

This design enforces self-consistency in the strongest possible sense.
Because the kernel is pure, replayable, and free of hidden state, the system
is always capable of reconstructing its own present from first principles.
There is no distinction between </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{how} the state was reached and
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{what} the state is; the former fully determines the latter.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Derived State and Non-Authority}

Although the kernel maintains derived structures—such as equivalence
classes, relation tables, and metadata maps—these structures are explicitly
non-authoritative. They exist only as caches that accelerate observation.
At any time, they may be discarded and reconstructed by replay.

This distinction is essential. In conventional systems, caches frequently
become sources of truth through optimization or necessity. In Spherepop OS,
by contrast, authority is rigidly centralized in the log. The kernel is
therefore immune to a wide class of synchronization errors and semantic
drift.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Kernel State Formalization}

To make these claims precise, we formalize the kernel state.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Kernel State]
A kernel state is a tuple
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span> = (O, U, R, M),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>O</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a finite set of object identifiers, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>U : O </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> O</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a
union--find parent map encoding equivalence, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>R </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subseteq</span></span><span> O </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span> O </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{T}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a set of typed relations, and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M : O </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{K}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rightharpoonup</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{V}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a partial metadata map.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

We write </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{rep}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>(o)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> for the canonical representative of object
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>o</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> under the equivalence relation induced by </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. Path compression is
applied deterministically during replay.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Operational Semantics of Events}

Kernel execution is defined by a labeled transition relation
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\xrightarrow</span></span><span>{e} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>',
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is an event drawn from a fixed and closed set of event types. Each
event type corresponds to a semantic commitment that refines the structure
of the world.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Object Introduction (POP).}
A POP event introduces a new semantic object into existence. The object has
no properties beyond its identity and initial self-representation under
equivalence. This reflects the principle that objects are inert until
related.

Formally, if </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>o </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\notin</span></span><span> O</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, then
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
(O, U, R, M) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\xrightarrow</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{POP}(o)}
(O </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cup</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span>o</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span> U[o </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mapsto</span></span><span> o],</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span> R,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span> M).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Equivalence Induction (MERGE).}
A MERGE event identifies two existing objects as equivalent. Importantly,
this operation does not destroy either object; instead, it collapses them
into a shared representative.

If </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>o</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1, o</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> O</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, then
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\xrightarrow</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{MERGE}(o</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1,o</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2)} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>',
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where the representative of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>o</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is redirected to that of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>o</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.

This operation is idempotent and confluent. Applying MERGE to already
equivalent objects has no effect, and the final equivalence relation is
independent of merge order. These properties ensure that abstraction is
stable under replay and resistant to race conditions.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proposition}[Confluence of Equivalence]
The equivalence relation induced by a set of MERGE events depends only on the
set of merges performed, not on their order.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proposition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proof}
Union--find computes the transitive closure of the merge relation, which is
order-independent.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proof}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Relation Construction (LINK and UNLINK).}
LINK events introduce directed, typed relations between canonical
representatives of objects. UNLINK events remove such relations. Both
operations are interpreted relative to current equivalence classes,
ensuring that abstraction propagates uniformly through relational structure.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Bulk Abstraction (COLLAPSE).}
The COLLAPSE event generalizes MERGE by inducing equivalence over an entire
region of objects. Conceptually, COLLAPSE corresponds to a deliberate act of
abstraction: many constructed entities are retrospectively identified as
instances of a single concept.

This operation is especially significant from the perspective of
intelligence theory. COLLAPSE does not reduce the number of events that
occurred; instead, it reduces the number of distinct representatives that
matter. As such, it exemplifies abstraction without compression, a
distinction that will become central later in the paper.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Metadata Attachment (SET</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\_</span></span><span>META).}
SET</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\_</span></span><span>META attaches auxiliary information to objects without affecting
semantic structure. Metadata is explicitly non-causal: it does not influence
equivalence, relations, or object existence.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{axiom}[Metadata Orthogonality]
Metadata updates commute with all kernel transitions and have no semantic
effect.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{axiom}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Determinism and Semantic Stability}

The operational semantics above satisfy strong determinism guarantees.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{theorem}[Kernel Determinism]
For any kernel state </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and event </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, there exists at most one state
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>'</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> such that </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\xrightarrow</span></span><span>{e} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>'</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{theorem}

This theorem underwrites every higher-level property of Spherepop OS. It
ensures that observers, regardless of vantage point or reconstruction
method, converge on the same semantic world when given the same history.

From the perspective articulated by Yi Ma, this determinism is not merely a
technical convenience but a prerequisite for intelligence. A system that
cannot reconstruct its own world consistently cannot form reliable models
of that world. By embedding determinism into its core, Spherepop OS enforces
self-consistency as an architectural invariant rather than a learned
behavior.

</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
