4.04237 6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-latex"><span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ==================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Observation, Diffs, and Non-Interference}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ==================================================</span></span><span>

While the authoritative event log determines all semantic state, a practical
system must permit observation without compromising determinism. Spherepop
OS resolves this tension by introducing a strict separation between
authoritative causation and non-authoritative observation.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Diff]
A diff is a derived, non-authoritative description of the semantic change
induced by applying a single event to a kernel state.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

Diffs are emitted by the kernel as observational artifacts. They are never
themselves logged, never replayed, and never consulted during future kernel
execution. Their sole purpose is to permit observers to maintain an
incremental view of semantic state without replaying the entire log.

This separation is enforced by construction rather than convention. The
kernel computes diffs as a pure function of state transition, but diffs have
no causal power. They may be dropped, delayed, reordered, or ignored by
observers without affecting the correctness of the system as a whole.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{axiom}[Observational Non-Interference]
Diffs have no effect on authoritative state and do not influence future
kernel execution.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{axiom}

This axiom eliminates an entire class of feedback-induced inconsistencies
that plague conventional systems. Observation is rendered epistemic rather
than ontological: it reveals what has occurred without altering what can
occur.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Incremental Reconstruction}

Observers may reconstruct semantic state incrementally by applying diffs in
sequence order. This reconstruction is semantically equivalent to full
replay.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proposition}[Incremental Equivalence]
Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be the kernel state obtained by replaying events
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dots</span></span><span>,e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> from the empty state. Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\hat</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be the observer
state obtained by applying diffs </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dots</span></span><span>,d</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. Then </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\hat</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> are semantically equivalent.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proposition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proof}
Each diff </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is deterministically derived from the kernel transition
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{i-1} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\xrightarrow</span></span><span>{e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. Because kernel transitions are
pure and diffs preserve all semantic information, incremental application
yields a state equivalent to replay.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proof}

This proposition ensures that no privileged reconstruction path exists.
Replay-based and diff-based observers converge on the same semantic world.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ==================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Snapshots as Derived Views}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ==================================================</span></span><span>

To support late-joining observers and historical inspection, Spherepop OS
permits the construction of snapshots.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Snapshot]
A snapshot is a complete serialization of kernel state derived by replaying
a prefix of the event log.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

Snapshots are derived artifacts, not authoritative records. They contain no
information that cannot be reconstructed from the corresponding log prefix.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{axiom}[Snapshot Non-Authority]
Snapshots introduce no new semantic information and have no effect on future
kernel execution.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{axiom}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proposition}[Snapshot Purity]
For any snapshot </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> derived from events </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>[1..k]</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, there exists a unique
kernel state </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> such that </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a faithful serialization of
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proposition}

Snapshots therefore serve as epistemic conveniences rather than ontological
commitments. They accelerate access but do not alter meaning.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Replay Equivalence}

The relationship between replay, diffs, and snapshots is captured by a
single unifying result.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{theorem}[Replay Equivalence]
Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ell</span></span><span> = e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dots</span></span><span>,e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be an event sequence. Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{replay}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>
be the state obtained by replaying </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ell</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> from the empty state. Let
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{incr}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be the state obtained by applying diffs
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dots</span></span><span>,d</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. Then </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{replay}} = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{incr}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> up
to semantic equivalence.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{theorem}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proof}
Both constructions apply the same deterministic kernel transitions in the
same order. Since diffs are derived views and introduce no additional
authority, the resulting states coincide.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proof}

This theorem guarantees global coherence across observers and reconstruction
strategies.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ==================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Seekable Time and Historical Reconstruction}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ==================================================</span></span><span>

A distinctive feature of Spherepop OS is explicit support for temporal
navigation. Rather than treating history as opaque or irreversible, the
system exposes time as a first-class query dimension.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Seek Operation]
A seek operation </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{seek}(k)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> constructs an ephemeral kernel state by
replaying the event log prefix </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>[1..k]</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

Seek operations instantiate temporary kernel instances that are isolated
from the live system. They permit historical inspection, debugging, and
counterfactual reasoning without side effects.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{axiom}[Temporal Isolation]
Seek operations produce no modifications to authoritative state and emit no
diffs.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{axiom}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{corollary}
Historical inspection cannot influence present or future semantic state.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{corollary}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Temporal Monotonicity}

The authoritative event sequence is strictly monotone. No operation may
decrease, rewrite, or fork the authoritative timeline.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proposition}[Temporal Monotonicity]
If </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>i &lt; j</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, then </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{eid}(e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i) &lt; </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{eid}(e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proposition}

This enforces a strong notion of causality: the past is immutable, and the
future is always constructed relative to a fixed history.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ==================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Self-Consistency and World Simulation}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ==================================================</span></span><span>

The architectural commitments described above jointly enforce a strong form
of self-consistency. At any moment, the system can reconstruct its entire
semantic state from first principles by replaying the event log.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{theorem}[Strong Self-Consistency]
For any time </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, the semantic state of Spherepop OS is uniquely determined
by the event log prefix </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>[1..t]</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{theorem}

This property directly instantiates the second foundational principle of
intelligence articulated by Yi Ma: a world model must be consistent enough
to support accurate simulation. In Spherepop OS, simulation is not an
approximation layered atop mutable state; it is an intrinsic consequence of
replay.

Because seek operations permit reconstruction of past states, and
speculative branches (introduced in the next section) permit exploration of
possible futures, the system possesses an internal model of its own
temporal evolution. This model operates below language and symbolism. It is
not a narrative or a description, but a constructive history that can be
replayed, inspected, and extended.

In this sense, Spherepop OS implements self-consistency not as a learning
objective but as a structural invariant. Any deviation from consistency
would manifest immediately as replay failure or divergence, rendering the
system epistemically unstable. By design, such instability is impossible.

</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
