</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\appendix</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Formal Distinction Between Compression and Abstraction}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ==================================================</span></span><span>

This appendix formalizes a distinction that is central to the theoretical
claims of the paper: the difference between compression and abstraction.
While these terms are often used interchangeably in the literature, they
correspond to distinct structural transformations in Spherepop OS.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Preliminaries}

Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ell</span></span><span> = (e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ldots</span></span><span>, e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be a prefix of the authoritative event log,
and let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ell</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> denote the kernel state obtained by replaying </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ell</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>
from the empty initial state.

Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>|</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ell</span></span><span>|</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> denote the length of the log prefix, and let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>|O(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ell</span></span><span>)|</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> denote
the number of object identifiers present in </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ell</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. Let
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>|U(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ell</span></span><span>)|</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> denote the number of equivalence classes induced by the union--
find structure.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Compression as Event Reduction}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Semantic Effect]
A semantic effect is an equivalence class of kernel states under
observational equivalence, where two states are observationally equivalent
if they induce the same canonical representatives and relations.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Compression]
Compression is the process of reducing the minimal log length required to
produce a given semantic effect.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

Formally, compression occurs when there exists a log prefix </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ell</span></span><span>'</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> such
that </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ell</span></span><span>')</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is observationally equivalent to </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ell</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>|</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ell</span></span><span>'| &lt; |</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ell</span></span><span>|</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.

Compression therefore operates on the temporal dimension of construction.
It removes redundant or unnecessary events while preserving semantic
outcomes.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Abstraction as Representative Reduction}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{definition}[Abstraction]
Abstraction is the process of reducing the number of distinct canonical
representatives required to account for a semantic effect.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{definition}

Formally, abstraction occurs when, for a fixed semantic effect,
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>|U(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ell</span></span><span>')| &lt; |U(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ell</span></span><span>)|</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, typically as the result of merge or collapse
events.

Abstraction operates on the structural dimension of the semantic field. It
does not shorten the log but simplifies the ontology induced by the log.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Independence of Compression and Abstraction}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proposition}
Compression and abstraction are logically independent operations.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proposition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proof}
Compression may occur without abstraction by removing redundant events that
introduce objects which are later unused, without altering equivalence
classes. Conversely, abstraction may occur without compression when merge
events increase log length while reducing the number of representatives.

Therefore, neither operation implies the other.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proof}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Visibility in the Log}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proposition}
The distinction between compression and abstraction is observable in the
event log and kernel state.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proposition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proof}
Compression is observable as a reduction in minimal log prefixes producing
a given semantic effect. Abstraction is observable as a reduction in the
cardinality of equivalence classes in </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ell</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. Both quantities are
explicitly computable from the log and kernel state.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proof}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Implications for Learning Systems}

This formal distinction explains why systems that optimize for compression
alone may fail to achieve abstraction. Statistical models that reduce data
volume without altering underlying representational commitments compress
signals without restructuring semantics.

By contrast, abstraction requires the system to identify and stabilize
equivalences across contexts, an operation that cannot be performed solely
at the level of surface compression.

Spherepop OS enforces this distinction architecturally, ensuring that
abstraction remains grounded in replayable construction rather than in
opaque parameter adjustment.

</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
<hr data-start="9466" data-end="9469">
<h3 data-start="9471" data-end="9492">What You Now Have</h3>
<ul data-start="9494" data-end="9820">
<li data-start="9494" data-end="9563">
<p data-start="9496" data-end="9563">A <strong data-start="9498" data-end="9532">field-theoretic interpretation</strong> that is rigorous, not poetic</p>
</li>
<li data-start="9564" data-end="9638">
<p data-start="9566" data-end="9638">A <strong data-start="9568" data-end="9599">formal proof-level appendix</strong> resolving compression vs abstraction</p>
</li>
<li data-start="9639" data-end="9714">
<p data-start="9641" data-end="9714">Explicit resolution of <strong data-start="9664" data-end="9688">Yi Ma’s open problem</strong> <em data-start="9689" data-end="9712">without redefining it</em></p>
</li>
<li data-start="9715" data-end="9776">
<p data-start="9717" data-end="9776">Strong alignment with cybernetics, cognition, and physics</p>
</li>
