4.04237 6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-latex"><span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ==================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Category-Theoretic Structure of Spherepop Semantics}
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ==================================================</span></span><span>

This appendix provides a categorical interpretation of the semantic
machinery of Spherepop OS. The goal is not abstraction for its own sake,
but to clarify which aspects of the system are structural invariants and
which are implementation artifacts.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Objects, Morphisms, and Construction Histories}

Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{C}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be a category whose objects correspond to semantic object
handles and whose morphisms correspond to construction relations induced
by events. Each event introduces either a new object, a new morphism, or a
constraint identifying existing objects.

The event log </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ell</span></span><span> = (e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ldots</span></span><span>, e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> induces a diagram </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>D</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ell</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> in
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{C}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> whose shape is determined by the dependency structure of
events. Replay corresponds to the progressive extension of this diagram.

Importantly, the category </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{C}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is not assumed to be small or
finitely generated a priori; its finiteness at any given time is an
emergent property of the log prefix.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{POP as Object Introduction}

A POP event introduces a new object </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>o</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> into the category, corresponding to
the free adjunction of an object. Categorically, this is a left adjoint to
the forgetful functor that removes isolated objects.

This interpretation clarifies why object creation is irreversible in the
authoritative log: left adjoints do not, in general, admit inverses.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{LINK as Morphism Introduction}

A LINK event introduces a morphism between two objects, enriching the
diagram without collapsing structure. Morphisms are directed and typed,
corresponding to labeled arrows in </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{C}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.

LINK events preserve object identity and do not alter equivalence classes.
They increase relational density without reducing dimensionality.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{MERGE and COLLAPSE as Coequalizers}

MERGE and COLLAPSE events impose equivalence relations between objects.
Categorically, these operations correspond to the formation of
coequalizers.

Given objects </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>o</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1, o</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> with parallel morphisms or semantic roles, a MERGE
event enforces their identification by introducing a coequalizer object
representing their quotient. COLLAPSE generalizes this to finite families.

The resulting quotient object is universal with respect to the imposed
identification, explaining the confluence and order-independence of merge
operations.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Semantic State as a Colimit}

For any log prefix </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ell</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, the kernel state </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ell</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> corresponds to
the colimit of the diagram </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>D</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ell</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. Replay computes this colimit
incrementally.

This perspective yields a concise explanation of determinism: colimits, if
they exist, are unique up to isomorphism. Different replay strategies
compute the same semantic object.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Views as Functorial Projections}

Derived views, snapshots, and diffs correspond to functors from
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{C}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> into categories of representations, such as graphs,
geometric spaces, or visual layouts.

These functors need not be faithful. Lossy projections are permitted,
provided they preserve semantic equivalence. This formalizes the principle
that views are observational and non-authoritative.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Speculation as Diagram Extension}

Speculative branches correspond to local extensions of the diagram
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>D</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ell</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> without committing to the colimit. Such extensions exist in a
slice category over the base diagram and may be discarded without
affecting the canonical colimit.

This explains why speculation is structurally safe: it never alters the
universal property defining semantic state.

</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
