"3f1fbb6c-3a00-4c65-9fa3-e8151d09580d" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-1" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5-2"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[1px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="314">Below is a <strong data-start="11" data-end="43">journal-specific outline map</strong> for the Spherepop / abstraction program, tuned to the <strong data-start="98" data-end="151">expectations, review culture, and epistemic norms</strong> of each venue. Each outline is a <em data-start="185" data-end="200">different cut</em> of the same core theory, not a rewrite—think of them as different gauge choices over the same semantic structure.</p>
<p data-start="316" data-end="437">I group them by <strong data-start="332" data-end="352">audience gravity</strong>: philosophy-forward, AI-theory, interdisciplinary cognition, and complexity/physics.</p>
<hr data-start="439" data-end="442">
<h2 data-start="444" data-end="472">1. <em data-start="450" data-end="470">Minds and Machines</em></h2>
<p data-start="473" data-end="519"><strong data-start="473" data-end="519">(Philosophy of AI / Cognitive Foundations)</strong></p>
<p data-start="521" data-end="541"><strong data-start="521" data-end="541">Reviewer mindset</strong></p>
<ul data-start="542" data-end="732">
<li data-start="542" data-end="601">
<p data-start="544" data-end="601">Wants conceptual clarity, necessity/sufficiency arguments</p>
</li>
<li data-start="602" data-end="669">
<p data-start="604" data-end="669">Skeptical of engineering details unless philosophically motivated</p>
</li>
<li data-start="670" data-end="732">
<p data-start="672" data-end="732">Very receptive to abstraction vs representation distinctions</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3 data-start="734" data-end="752">Title emphasis</h3>
<blockquote data-start="753" data-end="839">
<p data-start="755" data-end="839"><em data-start="755" data-end="839">Abstraction as Stabilized Replay: A Memory-First Account of Semantic Understanding</em></p>
</blockquote>
<h3 data-start="841" data-end="852">Outline</h3>
<p data-start="854" data-end="1018"><strong data-start="854" data-end="904">1. Introduction: The Abstraction Problem in AI</strong><br data-start="904" data-end="907">
– Why abstraction ≠ compression<br data-start="938" data-end="941">
– Why representation-first accounts fail<br data-start="981" data-end="984">
– Preview of memory-first solution</p>
<p data-start="1020" data-end="1152"><strong data-start="1020" data-end="1060">2. Historical Background (Selective)</strong><br data-start="1060" data-end="1063">
– Symbolic abstraction<br data-start="1085" data-end="1088">
– Statistical compression<br data-start="1113" data-end="1116">
– Why both miss construction history</p>
<p data-start="1154" data-end="1302"><strong data-start="1154" data-end="1190">3. Ontology of Events and Memory</strong><br data-start="1190" data-end="1193">
– Events as semantic commitments<br data-start="1225" data-end="1228">
– Time and irreversibility<br data-start="1254" data-end="1257">
– Why memory is ontological, not instrumental</p>
<p data-start="1304" data-end="1451"><strong data-start="1304" data-end="1330">4. Replay as Reasoning</strong><br data-start="1330" data-end="1333">
– Deterministic replay vs inference<br data-start="1368" data-end="1371">
– Counterfactuals as modified histories<br data-start="1410" data-end="1413">
– Explanation as projection, not cause</p>
<p data-start="1453" data-end="1624"><strong data-start="1453" data-end="1487">5. Equivalence and Abstraction</strong><br data-start="1487" data-end="1490">
– Semantic equivalence defined<br data-start="1520" data-end="1523">
– Abstraction as representative collapse<br data-start="1563" data-end="1566">
– Difference between fewer events vs fewer representatives</p>
<p data-start="1626" data-end="1780"><strong data-start="1626" data-end="1658">6. Stability Under Variation</strong><br data-start="1658" data-end="1661">
– What it means for an abstraction to “hold”<br data-start="1705" data-end="1708">
– Perturbation classes<br data-start="1730" data-end="1733">
– Semantic depth (conceptual, not mathematical)</p>
<p data-start="1782" data-end="1964"><strong data-start="1782" data-end="1826">7. Why Compression Alone Is Insufficient</strong><br data-start="1826" data-end="1829">
– Language as already-compressed signal<br data-start="1868" data-end="1871">
– Memorization without abstraction<br data-start="1905" data-end="1908">
– Structural explanation of LLM limitations (no polemic)</p>
<p data-start="1966" data-end="2134"><strong data-start="1966" data-end="2012">8. Implications for Understanding and Mind</strong><br data-start="2012" data-end="2015">
– Understanding as stabilized semantic replay<br data-start="2060" data-end="2063">
