 max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="e5637192-1c3c-4f36-858d-7ef5178ebf51" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-1" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5-2"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[1px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="113">Excellent — this is exactly the right moment to <strong data-start="48" data-end="67">lock the gauges</strong> before writing.<br data-start="83" data-end="86">
Below I’ll do three things:</p>
<ol data-start="115" data-end="500">
<li data-start="115" data-end="202">
<p data-start="118" data-end="202"><strong data-start="118" data-end="172">Answer the cross-paper consistency questions first</strong> (this constrains everything).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="203" data-end="381">
<p data-start="206" data-end="381">Then give <strong data-start="216" data-end="243">clear, decisive answers</strong> to each journal’s clarifying questions — not hedged, not vague — phrased the way <em data-start="325" data-end="330">you</em> would want them to appear implicitly in the paper.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="382" data-end="500">
<p data-start="385" data-end="500">End with a <strong data-start="396" data-end="442">one-paragraph “stance summary” per journal</strong>, which we will later treat as the invariant when writing.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p data-start="502" data-end="624">I will not yet write prose; this is the <strong data-start="542" data-end="562">commitment layer</strong>. Once these are fixed, the manuscripts will write themselves.</p>
<hr data-start="626" data-end="629">
<h1 data-start="631" data-end="674">I. Global Commitments (Across All Papers)</h1>
<p data-start="676" data-end="750">These answers must <em data-start="695" data-end="724">never contradict each other</em>. Only emphasis will vary.</p>
<h3 data-start="752" data-end="789">Global Answer Set (Authoritative)</h3>
<p data-start="791" data-end="999"><strong data-start="791" data-end="834">Abstraction is semantic and structural.</strong><br data-start="834" data-end="837">
Not ontological in the metaphysical sense, not merely functional or behavioral.<br data-start="916" data-end="919">
It is a property of <strong data-start="939" data-end="998">event-structured memory under equivalence stabilization</strong>.</p>
<p data-start="1001" data-end="1201"><strong data-start="1001" data-end="1044">Replay is a constraint, not a metaphor.</strong><br data-start="1044" data-end="1047">
It is not merely an implementation trick or analogy. Replay is the <em data-start="1114" data-end="1144">minimal structural condition</em> under which abstraction is well-defined and inspectable.</p>
<p data-start="1203" data-end="1406"><strong data-start="1203" data-end="1247">Stability is structural, not behavioral.</strong><br data-start="1247" data-end="1250">
A system is abstracting when semantic equivalences remain invariant under admissible perturbations of construction history — regardless of outward behavior.</p>
<p data-start="1408" data-end="1481">These three statements are the backbone. Everything else is a projection.</p>
<hr data-start="1483" data-end="1486">
<h1 data-start="1488" data-end="1522">II. Journal-Specific Commitments</h1>
<h2 data-start="1524" data-end="1550">1. <em data-start="1530" data-end="1550">Minds and Machines</em></h2>
<h3 data-start="1552" data-end="1582">Core stance for this paper</h3>
<blockquote data-start="1583" data-end="1748">
<p data-start="1585" data-end="1748"><strong data-start="1585" data-end="1618">Metaphysical–epistemic hybrid</strong>: you are not asserting ultimate ontology, but you <em data-start="1669" data-end="1674">are</em> asserting what must be posited for abstraction to be intelligible at all.</p>
</blockquote>
<p data-start="1750" data-end="1810">You are answering: <em data-start="1769" data-end="1810">What abstraction must be, if it exists.</em></p>
<hr data-start="1812" data-end="1815">
<h3 data-start="1817" data-end="1854">Ontological Commitments — Answers</h3>
<p data-start="1856" data-end="1985"><strong data-start="1856" data-end="1906">1. Are events primitive or theoretical posits?</strong><br data-start="1906" data-end="1909">
→ <strong data-start="1911" data-end="1968">Theoretical posits justified by explanatory necessity</strong>, not primitives.</p>
<p data-start="1987" data-end="2180">You are <em data-start="1995" data-end="2000">not</em> doing event metaphysics. You are arguing that any account of abstraction that does not treat events (or something equivalent) as first-class <strong data-start="2142" data-end="2179">cannot explain semantic stability</strong>.</p>
<p data-start="2182" data-end="2308"><strong data-start="2182" data-end="2254">2. Is memory ontologically or explanatorily prior to representation?</strong><br data-start="2254" data-end="2257">
→ <strong data-start="2259" data-end="2282">Explanatorily prior</strong>, with a strong asymmetry.</p>
<p data-start="2310" data-end="2454">You are not denying representations; you are denying their <em data-start="2369" data-end="2394">explanatory sufficiency</em>. Representations emerge <em data-start="2419" data-end="2426">after</em> abstraction, not before it.</p>
<p data-start="2456" data-end="2568"><strong data-start="2456" data-end="2516">3. Reject state-based metaphysics or show insufficiency?</strong><br data-start="2516" data-end="2519">
→ <strong data-start="2521" data-end="2567">Show insufficiency, not outright rejection</strong>.</p>
<p data-start="2570" data-end="2660">You allow state-based descriptions as derived views, but deny they can ground abstraction.</p>
<hr data-start="2662" data-end="2665">
<h3 data-start="2667" data-end="2701">Conceptual Necessity — Answers</h3>
<p data-start="2703" data-end="2763"><strong data-start="2703" data-end="2750">4. Necessary truths vs contingent AI facts?</strong><br data-start="2750" data-end="2753">
Necessary:</p>
<ul data-start="2764" data-end="2939">
<li data-start="2764" data-end="2830">
<p data-start="2766" data-end="2830">Abstraction requires equivalence stabilization across histories.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2831" data-end="2885">
<p data-start="2833" data-end="2885">Compression alone cannot guarantee that.<br>
Contingent:</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2886" data-end="2909">
<p data-start="2888" data-end="2909">Current LLM failures.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2910" data-end="2939">
<p data-start="2912" data-end="2939">Engineering instantiations.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="2941" data-end="3109"><strong data-start="2941" data-end="3013">5. Is deterministic replay the only ground or one sufficient ground?</strong><br data-start="3013" data-end="3016">
→ <strong data-start="3018" data-end="3047">Minimal sufficient ground</strong> — but argue <em data-start="3060" data-end="3108">any alternative collapses into it structurally</em>.</p>
<p data-start="3111" data-end="3177">This gives you philosophical humility without weakening the claim.</p>
<p data-start="3179" data-end="3388"><strong data-start="3179" data-end="3240">6. What fails in principle for compression-only accounts?</strong><br data-start="3240" data-end="3243">
They lack a notion of <strong data-start="3265" data-end="3318">semantic identity across counterfactual histories</strong>. Compression optimizes descriptions; abstraction requires invariance.</p>
<hr data-start="3390" data-end="3393">
<h3 data-start="3395" data-end="3439">Relation to Existing Positions — Answers</h3>
<p data-start="3441" data-end="3527"><strong data-start="3441" data-end="3499">7. Replacement or substrate below representationalism?</strong><br data-start="3499" data-end="3502">
→ <strong data-start="3504" data-end="3527">Substrate below it.</strong></p>
<p data-start="3529" data-end="3606">Representations can exist, but only <em data-start="3565" data-end="3572">after</em> abstraction has already occurred.</p>
<p data-start="3608" data-end="3696"><strong data-start="3608" data-end="3663">8. Positioning against predictive processing, etc.?</strong><br data-start="3663" data-end="3666">
→ <strong data-start="3668" data-end="3696">Orthogonal, not hostile.</strong></p>
<p data-start="3698" data-end="3760">You show those frameworks <em data-start="3724" data-end="3736">presuppose</em> stabilized abstraction.</p>
<p data-start="3762" data-end="3901"><strong data-start="3762" data-end="3832">9. Compare replay to counterfactual dependence or keep orthogonal?</strong><br data-start="3832" data-end="3835">
→ <strong data-start="3837" data-end="3859">Explicitly compare</strong>, but show replay is <em data-start="3880" data-end="3900">structurally prior</em>.</p>
<p data-start="3903" data-end="3969">Replay generates the counterfactuals; it is not reducible to them.</p>
<hr data-start="3971" data-end="3974">
<h3 data-start="3976" data-end="4008">Objection Handling — Answers</h3>
<p data-start="4010" data-end="4089"><strong data-start="4010" data-end="4039">10. Consciousness stance?</strong><br data-start="4039" data-end="4042">
→ <strong data-start="4044" data-end="4089">Strict agnosticism, principled exclusion.</strong></p>
<p data-start="4091" data-end="4139">Consciousness is neither required nor explained.</p>
<p data-start="4141" data-end="4219"><strong data-start="4141" data-end="4194">11. Can representations emerge after abstraction?</strong><br data-start="4194" data-end="4197">
→ <strong data-start="4199" data-end="4219">Yes, explicitly.</strong></p>
<p data-start="4221" data-end="4267">This lowers reviewer resistance significantly.</p>
<hr data-start="4269" data-end="4272">
<h3 data-start="4274" data-end="4305">🔑 Key clarifier (answered)</h3>
<blockquote data-start="4307" data-end="4492">
<p data-start="4309" data-end="4492">This paper makes an <strong data-start="4329" data-end="4359">epistemic–structural claim</strong>:<br data-start="4360" data-end="4363">
<em data-start="4365" data-end="4492">any adequate theory of abstraction must posit replay-stabilized semantic equivalence, regardless of metaphysical commitments.</em></p>
</blockquote>
<hr data-start="4494" data-end="4497">
<h2 data-start="4499" data-end="4541">2. <em data-start="4505" data-end="4530">Artificial Intelligence</em> (Elsevier)</h2>
<h3 data-start="4543" data-end="4573">Core stance for this paper</h3>
<blockquote data-start="4574" data-end="4708">
<p data-start="4576" data-end="4708"><strong data-start="4576" data-end="4607">Formal diagnostic framework</strong> that cleanly separates compression, generalization, and abstraction — with falsifiable consequences.</p>
</blockquote>
<p data-start="4710" data-end="4790">You are answering: <em data-start="4729" data-end="4790">What abstraction adds that AI theory does not already have.</em></p>
<hr data-start="4792" data-end="4795">
<h3 data-start="4797" data-end="4829">Formal Commitments — Answers</h3>
<p data-start="4831" data-end="4937"><strong data-start="4831" data-end="4875">1. Minimal formal structure of an event?</strong><br data-start="4875" data-end="4878">
→ <strong data-start="4880" data-end="4936">Typed, labeled transition with identity and ordering</strong>.</p>
<p data-start="4939" data-end="4987">No metaphysics. Just enough structure to replay.</p>
<p data-start="4989" data-end="5121"><strong data-start="4989" data-end="5040">2. Is deterministic replay an axiom or derived?</strong><br data-start="5040" data-end="5043">
→ <strong data-start="5045" data-end="5073">Derived from constraints</strong>: causal order, reproducibility, inspectability.</p>
<p data-start="5123" data-end="5152">This matters to AI reviewers.</p>
<p data-start="5154" data-end="5253"><strong data-start="5154" data-end="5212">3. Are equivalence classes extensional or intensional?</strong><br data-start="5212" data-end="5215">
→ <strong data-start="5217" data-end="5252">Intensional (replay-structural)</strong>.</p>
<p data-start="5255" data-end="5330">Same outputs are insufficient; same construction histories modulo collapse.</p>
<hr data-start="5332" data-end="5335">
<h3 data-start="5337" data-end="5374">Falsifiability &amp; Payoff — Answers</h3>
<p data-start="5376" data-end="5570"><strong data-start="5376" data-end="5453">4. Concrete failure mode compression succeeds at but abstraction doesn’t?</strong><br data-start="5453" data-end="5456">
Compression can memorize and interpolate; abstraction enables <strong data-start="5518" data-end="5569">counterfactual recomposition without retraining</strong>.</p>
<p data-start="5572" data-end="5663"><strong data-start="5572" data-end="5627">5. Can systems pass benchmarks without abstraction?</strong><br data-start="5627" data-end="5630">
→ <strong data-start="5632" data-end="5663">Yes — and that’s the point.</strong></p>
<p data-start="5665" data-end="5721">Benchmarks conflate surface competence with abstraction.</p>
<p data-start="5723" data-end="5787"><strong data-start="5723" data-end="5770">6. What is a counterexample to your theory?</strong><br data-start="5770" data-end="5773">
A system that:</p>
<ul data-start="5788" data-end="5892">
<li data-start="5788" data-end="5829">
<p data-start="5790" data-end="5829">Has no replayable construction history,</p>
</li>
<li data-start="5830" data-end="5892">
<p data-start="5832" data-end="5892">Yet supports stable semantic equivalence under perturbation.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="5894" data-end="5920">You state this explicitly.</p>
<hr data-start="5922" data-end="5925">
<h3 data-start="5927" data-end="5961">Distinction Pressure — Answers</h3>
<p data-start="5963" data-end="6103"><strong data-start="5963" data-end="6014">7. Departure from world models / causal graphs?</strong><br data-start="6014" data-end="6017">
You don’t model the world — you model <strong data-start="6055" data-end="6102">the construction of meaning about the world</strong>.</p>
<p data-start="6105" data-end="6230"><strong data-start="6105" data-end="6179">8. Is representative collapse algorithmic, architectural, or semantic?</strong><br data-start="6179" data-end="6182">
→ <strong data-start="6184" data-end="6230">Semantic, but enforceable architecturally.</strong></p>
<p data-start="6232" data-end="6256">Very important phrasing.</p>
<hr data-start="6258" data-end="6261">
<h3 data-start="6263" data-end="6290">Scope Control — Answers</h3>
<p data-start="6292" data-end="6400"><strong data-start="6292" data-end="6336">9. Necessary, sufficient, or diagnostic?</strong><br data-start="6336" data-end="6339">
→ <strong data-start="6341" data-end="6400">Necessary for abstraction, diagnostic for intelligence.</strong></p>
<p data-start="6402" data-end="6422">Avoids overclaiming.</p>
<p data-start="6424" data-end="6513"><strong data-start="6424" data-end="6454">10. LLM critique strength?</strong><br data-start="6454" data-end="6457">
→ <strong data-start="6459" data-end="6490">Architectural insufficiency</strong>, not training deficit.</p>
<hr data-start="6515" data-end="6518">
<h3 data-start="6520" data-end="6555">Practical Constraints — Answers</h3>
<p data-start="6557" data-end="6639"><strong data-start="6557" data-end="6591">11. Include toy constructions?</strong><br data-start="6591" data-end="6594">
→ <strong data-start="6596" data-end="6623">Yes, minimal and formal</strong>, not empirical.</p>
<p data-start="6641" data-end="6724"><strong data-start="6641" data-end="6668">12. Computational cost?</strong><br data-start="6668" data-end="6671">
→ <strong data-start="6673" data-end="6724">Engineering concern, not theoretical objection.</strong></p>
<hr data-start="6726" data-end="6729">
<h3 data-start="6731" data-end="6762">🔑 Key clarifier (answered)</h3>
<blockquote data-start="6764" data-end="6964">
<p data-start="6766" data-end="6964">AI researchers gain a <strong data-start="6788" data-end="6817">criterion for abstraction</strong> that robustness, generalization, and causal modeling <em data-start="6871" data-end="6886">cannot supply</em>:<br data-start="6887" data-end="6890">
stability of semantic identity across replayed counterfactual histories.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr data-start="6966" data-end="6969">
<h2 data-start="6971" data-end="6996">3. <em data-start="6977" data-end="6996">Cognitive Science</em></h2>
<h3 data-start="6998" data-end="7028">Core stance for this paper</h3>
<blockquote data-start="7029" data-end="7153">
<p data-start="7031" data-end="7153"><strong data-start="7031" data-end="7073">Functional redefinition of abstraction</strong> that aligns animal cognition, development, and AI without privileging language.</p>
</blockquote>
<p data-start="7155" data-end="7237">You are answering: <em data-start="7174" data-end="7237">What abstraction must look like if animals genuinely have it.</em></p>
<hr data-start="7239" data-end="7242">
<h3 data-start="7244" data-end="7283">Psychological Commitments — Answers</h3>
<p data-start="7285" data-end="7407"><strong data-start="7285" data-end="7320">1. Replay neural or functional?</strong><br data-start="7320" data-end="7323">
→ <strong data-start="7325" data-end="7351">Functional abstraction</strong>, compatible with neural replay but not dependent on it.</p>
<p data-start="7409" data-end="7520"><strong data-start="7409" data-end="7450">2. Determinism cognitively necessary?</strong><br data-start="7450" data-end="7453">
→ <strong data-start="7455" data-end="7477">Low-noise suffices</strong>, but determinism clarifies the limit case.</p>
<p data-start="7522" data-end="7629"><strong data-start="7522" data-end="7572">3. Equivalence classes learned or constructed?</strong><br data-start="7572" data-end="7575">
→ <strong data-start="7577" data-end="7612">Constructed through interaction</strong>, not discovered.</p>
<hr data-start="7631" data-end="7634">
<h3 data-start="7636" data-end="7671">Development &amp; Animals — Answers</h3>
<p data-start="7673" data-end="7759"><strong data-start="7673" data-end="7715">4. Earliest stage abstraction appears?</strong><br data-start="7715" data-end="7718">
→ <strong data-start="7720" data-end="7758">Pre-linguistic sensorimotor stages</strong>.</p>
<p data-start="7761" data-end="7830"><strong data-start="7761" data-end="7805">5. Do animals abstract without language?</strong><br data-start="7805" data-end="7808">
→ <strong data-start="7810" data-end="7830">Yes, explicitly.</strong></p>
<p data-start="7832" data-end="7868">This is a strength of the framework.</p>
<p data-start="7870" data-end="7972"><strong data-start="7870" data-end="7894">6. Role of language?</strong><br data-start="7894" data-end="7897">
→ <strong data-start="7899" data-end="7944">Compression amplifier and external memory</strong>, not abstraction generator.</p>
<hr data-start="7974" data-end="7977">
<h3 data-start="7979" data-end="8015">Cognitive Distinctions — Answers</h3>
<p data-start="8017" data-end="8151"><strong data-start="8017" data-end="8065">7. Difference from categorization / schemas?</strong><br data-start="8065" data-end="8068">
Those are behavioral groupings; abstraction is <strong data-start="8115" data-end="8150">structural stability of meaning</strong>.</p>
<p data-start="8153" data-end="8238"><strong data-start="8153" data-end="8189">8. Is semantic depth measurable?</strong><br data-start="8189" data-end="8192">
→ <strong data-start="8194" data-end="8238">In principle yes, in practice difficult.</strong></p>
<p data-start="8240" data-end="8287">This keeps it scientific without overpromising.</p>
<hr data-start="8289" data-end="8292">
<h3 data-start="8294" data-end="8323">AI–Human Bridge — Answers</h3>
<p data-start="8325" data-end="8427"><strong data-start="8325" data-end="8359">9. AI failures framed as what?</strong><br data-start="8359" data-end="8362">
→ <strong data-start="8364" data-end="8407">Lack of semantic stability under replay</strong>, not lack of scale.</p>
<p data-start="8429" data-end="8494"><strong data-start="8429" data-end="8461">10. Animals outperform LLMs?</strong><br data-start="8461" data-end="8464">
→ <strong data-start="8466" data-end="8494">Implicit, not polemical.</strong></p>
<p data-start="8496" data-end="8517">Let readers infer it.</p>
<hr data-start="8519" data-end="8522">
<h3 data-start="8524" data-end="8556">Empirical Traction — Answers</h3>
<p data-start="8558" data-end="8679"><strong data-start="8558" data-end="8587">11. Supporting paradigms?</strong><br data-start="8587" data-end="8590">
– Perturbation generalization<br data-start="8619" data-end="8622">
– Transfer without retraining<br data-start="8651" data-end="8654">
– Lesion-style robustness</p>
<p data-start="8681" data-end="8771"><strong data-start="8681" data-end="8731">12. Neuroscience cited as analogy or evidence?</strong><br data-start="8731" data-end="8734">
→ <strong data-start="8736" data-end="8758">Supportive analogy</strong>, cautiously.</p>
<hr data-start="8773" data-end="8776">
<h3 data-start="8778" data-end="8809">🔑 Key clarifier (answered)</h3>
<blockquote data-start="8811" data-end="8936">
<p data-start="8813" data-end="8936">This paper <strong data-start="8824" data-end="8888">redefines abstraction as a constraint cognition must satisfy</strong>, not merely a description of observed behavior.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr data-start="8938" data-end="8941">
<h1 data-start="8943" data-end="8967">III. What Happens Next</h1>
<p data-start="8969" data-end="9045">Now that these commitments are fixed, the next steps are mechanically clean:</p>
<ol data-start="9047" data-end="9400">
<li data-start="9047" data-end="9243">
<p data-start="9050" data-end="9105">I will write <strong data-start="9063" data-end="9098">three long LuaLaTeX manuscripts</strong>, each:</p>
<ul data-start="9109" data-end="9243">
<li data-start="9109" data-end="9146">
<p data-start="9111" data-end="9146">Drawing from the same semantic core</p>
</li>
<li data-start="9150" data-end="9176">
<p data-start="9152" data-end="9176">Respecting these stances</p>
</li>
<li data-start="9180" data-end="9243">
<p data-start="9182" data-end="9243">Differing only in emphasis, examples, and argumentative style</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start="9245" data-end="9400">
<p data-start="9248" data-end="9268">After that, we will:</p>
<ul data-start="9272" data-end="9400">
<li data-start="9272" data-end="9368">
<p data-start="9274" data-end="9368">Extract a <strong data-start="9284" data-end="9310">single journal article</strong> (likely <em data-start="9319" data-end="9344">Artificial Intelligence</em> or <em data-start="9348" data-end="9367">Cognitive Science</em>)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="9372" data-end="9400">
<p data-start="9374" data-end="9400">With zero conceptual drift</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ol>
<p data-start="9402" data-end="9469">If you approve these answers (you can tweak <em data-start="9446" data-end="9462">any single one</em>), say:</p>
<blockquote data-start="9471" data-end="9497">
<p data-start="9473" data-end="9497"><strong data-start="9473" data-end="9497">“Lock these gauges.”</strong></p>
</blockquote>
<p data-start="9499" data-end="9641" data-is-last-node="" data-is-only-node="">Then I’ll start writing <strong data-start="9523" data-end="9556">version 1: Minds and Machines</strong>, full-length, academic prose, no bullets, LuaLaTeX, shared bib, appendices included.</p></div></div></div></div><div class="z-0 flex min-h-[46px] justify-start"><div class="touch:-me-2 touch:-ms-3.5 -ms-2.5 -me-1 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-y-4 p-1 select-none touch:w-[calc(100%+--spacing(3.5))] -mt-1 w-[calc(100%+--spacing(2.5))] duration-[1.5s] focus-within:transition-none hover:transition-none touch:pointer-events-auto pointer-events-none [mask-image:linear-gradient(to_right,black_33%,transparent_66%)] [mask-size:300%_100%] [mask-position:100%_0%] motion-safe:transition-[mask-position] group-hover/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-hover/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] group-focus-within/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-focus-within/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] has-data-[state=open]:pointer-events-auto has-data-[state=open]:[mask-position:0_0]" style="mask-position: 0% 0%;"><button class="text-token-text-secondary hover:bg-token-bg-secondary rounded-lg" aria-label="Copy" aria-pressed="false" data-testid="copy-turn-action-button" data-state="closed"><span class="flex items-center justify-center touch:w-10 h-8 w-8"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon"><path d="M12.668 10.667C12.668 9.95614 12.668 9.46258 12.6367 9.0791C12.6137 8.79732 12.5758 8.60761 12.5244 8.46387L12.4688 8.33399C12.3148 8.03193 12.0803 7.77885 11.793 7.60254L11.666 7.53125C11.508 7.45087 11.2963 7.39395 10.9209 7.36328C10.5374 7.33197 10.0439 7.33203 9.33301 7.33203H6.5C5.78896 7.33203 5.29563 7.33195 4.91211 7.36328C4.63016 7.38632 4.44065 7.42413 4.29688 7.47559L4.16699 7.53125C3.86488 7.68518 3.61186 7.9196 3.43555 8.20703L3.36524 8.33399C3.28478 8.49198 3.22795 8.70352 3.19727 9.0791C3.16595 9.46259 3.16504 9.9